@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-test-suites Test suites
@brief Test suites are named groups of related test cases.

@details
A suite has a name and a body, a function that creates its test cases.
The suites are either added in `main()` with `runner::add_suite()`, or
defined as static suites, and `runner::run_suites()` runs them all, sorted
by name.

The top suite is a shortcut: a suite whose body is the code of `main()`
itself. `runner::add_top_suite()` adds it and returns it, and the test
cases created on it in `main()` run immediately, before
`runner::run_suites()` is called. It therefore always runs before all the
other suites, whatever their names. It is optional; a program may put all
its test cases in the other suites.

A test case can be created only on the suite whose body is running: on
the top suite from `main()`, or on the suite received by the body of
another suite. A call on another suite, for example on the top suite
captured in the body of another suite, is reported as a failure, and the
test case is not run.

The static suites are `mt::static_suite` objects, defined at namespace
scope, usually in separate source files, and constructed with a name, a
reference to the runner, and a callable (usually a function or a lambda
with a `mt::suite&` first parameter, which creates the test cases), along
with any optional arguments. They register themselves in their
constructors, so they are included in the test run without other calls.

@important
The runner referred to by the static suites in other source files must
have external linkage (not `static`), and be declared `extern` in a
header shared by these files.

A static suite does not need the runner to be constructed first; it can
be defined in any source file, before or after the runner.

@par Execution order
The top suite runs first, as described above. `runner::run_suites()`
then runs all the other suites, the added and the static ones together,
sorted by name:
- the names are compared byte by byte, as by `std::strcmp()`, so the
  upper-case letters come before the lower-case ones (`"Gamma"` runs
  before `"alpha"`);
- suites with the same name run in the order in which they were created;
  the static suites are created before `main()` starts, so they run
  before the added suites with the same name;
- the order of static suites with the same name, defined in different
  source files, is not specified; give them distinct names.

To set the order explicitly, start the names with zero-padded numbers
(`"01 - Init"`, `"02 - Run"`). Even so, write each suite so that it does
not depend on the others having run before it.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
// In a header shared by the source files.
extern micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner tr;
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

// In main.cpp: the runner, with external linkage.
mt::runner tr;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  tr.initialise (argc, argv);

  auto& ts = tr.add_top_suite ("Sample");

  ts.test ("Check one", [] (auto& t) {
    t.expect (true) << "Passed";
  });

  return tr.run_suites ();
}
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

// In a separate source file: a self-registering suite.
static mt::static_suite ts_extra
    = { "Extra suite", tr, [] (mt::suite& ts) {
  ts.test ("Check two", [] (auto& t) { t.expect (true) << "Passed"; });
} };

// Parametrised test suite accepting additional arguments.
static void
test_suite_with_args (mt::suite& ts, int ic, int iv,
                      int& ir, int* ip1, int* ip2)
{
  ts.test ("args", [&] (auto& t) {
    t.expect (mt::eq (ic, 42)) << "ic is 42";
    t.expect (mt::eq (iv, 43)) << "iv is 43";
    t.expect (mt::eq (ir, 44)) << "ir is 44";
    t.expect (mt::eq (*ip1, 45)) << "*ip1 is 45";
    t.expect (mt::eq (*ip2, 46)) << "*ip2 is 46";
  });
}

static int in = 43;
static int in44 = 44;
static int& ir = in44;
static int in45 = 45;
static int in46 = 46;
static int* ip2 = &in46;

static mt::static_suite ts_args
    = { "Args suite", tr, test_suite_with_args,
        42, in, ir, &in45, ip2 };
@endcode
